# death_if_fails rescaled for real per-act risk (drafts/repeats/rescale_death.py)
# Chroma library, modern Earth: PASTIMES and the small pleasures of ordinary life (next batch, drafts/next2/).
# Emren's point 11 ("drugs, fishing... There are lots of things that character can interact") and point 15 ("appreciate
# this glorious complexity and simplicity"). Twenty everyday moments, child to elder. Each calls two colors; over the
# file each of the ten pairs is called twice, and ten moments span two neighbouring stages, so every stage meets exactly
# five moments and calls each color twice (each stage even in calls, means, ends and chance). Cross cycles s1 to s4
# five times each.
# Syntax: header of build.py. Rules: library-spec.md. Brief: drafts/next2/BRIEF.md.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- child: calls WG (cycle s1)

# gate: a grandparent alive and within reach (the cast fills {grandparent}); the format has no field for it
== a first fish on a grandparent's line
tier: everyday
stages: child
age: 8-12
calls: WG
once: yes
tone: joy
life: family, nature, play
horizon: moment
roles: grandparent, sibling, the old men on the next peg
earth: your grandparent takes you fishing at dawn, and the float goes under for the first time
times: Some children, once; perhaps 1 in 5 are taken fishing by a grandparent (estimate); about 1 in 5 Americans aged 6 and over went fishing in 2023 (RBFF)
share: .2
rate: .5
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: {grandparent} and {N} sit on upturned buckets at the river's edge at six in the morning, a tin of maggots between them and a flask of sweet tea going cold. The float bobs, and bobs, and then it is gone.
scene W: {grandparent} gave the rules in the car: no shouting, wet hands before touching a fish, and the little ones go back. {N} has said them over and over all the way to the river.
scene U: The float, the split shot, a hook no bigger than a fingernail: {N} wants to know why each one sits where it does, and why the fish come to this bend and not the next.
scene B: {sibling} caught a perch last summer and has not stopped talking about it since. {N} means to come home with something bigger.
scene R: The rod tip dips, the reel gives a thin scream, and {Ns} heart goes so fast that every rule from the car flies straight out of their head.
scene G: Mist lies on the water, a heron stands in the shallows, and the river smells of mud and weed. {grandparent} fished this same bend as a child, beside their own grandfather.
- W | wet your hands, unhook it the way you were shown, and slip it back | v: conformity, universalism (nature) | chance: 70
- U | ask what every knot and weight is for, then tie the next hook yourself | door | v: self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 65
- B | tell everyone at home it was twice the size it really was | v: power, achievement | mark: hid a wrong | chance: 75
- R | heave the rod up so hard the fish flies over your head into the grass | body: light | v: stimulation | chance: 58
- G | sit still all morning and watch the water the way your grandparent does | v: tradition, universalism (nature) | mark: learned a skill | self_control: + | grants: hunting and fishing | chance: 65
- W>U | do each step exactly as you are shown, and say it back out loud | v: conformity, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 72
- U>B | watch where the old men on the next peg cast, and quietly cast there too | v: achievement | chance: 75
- B>R | promise to wash up all week if you can stay another hour | v: hedonism, stimulation | act: promise to wash up all week if they can stay another hour | chance: 68
- R>G | let the fish go yourself, in tears, and wave it off down the river | v: universalism (nature), benevolence | chance: 82
- G>W | carry the trout home for supper and give everyone at the table a piece | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 75
works: The fish comes out of the water silver and kicking, and {grandparent} laughs out loud.
works: {N} goes home smelling of river and maggots, and tells the story four times before bed.
fails: The line goes slack, and the hook comes back bare with the bait gone.
fails: The fish twists off at the bank, and {N} is quiet all the way back to the car.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- child and juvenile: calls UG (cycle s2)

== the garden bird count
tier: everyday
stages: child juvenile
age: 5-14
calls: UG
tone: joy
life: home, nature, school
horizon: moment
roles: parent, sibling, grandparent, a teacher
earth: one hour at the kitchen window on a January weekend, counting the birds for the national survey
times: Some children, once or a few times, often through school; over half a million people a year count garden birds for the RSPB Big Garden Birdwatch
gap: 2-4
rate: .5
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: It is the weekend of the big bird count, and {N} kneels on a kitchen chair at the window with a pencil, a tally sheet from school and one hour to fill it.
scene W: The sheet is very clear: one hour only, and for each kind, only the most seen at one time. The kitchen clock is there to keep {N} honest.
scene U: Coal tit, great tit, marsh tit: they all look the same until suddenly they do not. The bird book lies open on the sill at the page of little ones.
scene B: The teacher said the longest honest list in the class wins the job of feeding the guinea pig for a week. {N} wants that job badly.
scene R: Ten minutes in there has been one pigeon, and {N} is squirming on the chair with their nose squashed against the cold glass.
scene G: Frost on the lawn, crumbs on the feeder, and a robin on the fence post as if it owns the whole garden. {N} breathes a cloud on the glass and waits.
- W | count only what you see, for exactly one hour by the kitchen clock | v: conformity | chance: 85
- U | look up every bird in the book until you can tell the tits apart | habit | v: self-direction, achievement | mark: learned a skill | chance: 70
- B | add a woodpecker to the sheet that you never saw | v: achievement, power | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: a made-up sighting; backfire: the teacher asks for a drawing of it, and the story comes apart | chance: 65
- R | give up on the window and run out to chase the pigeons off the lawn | body: light | v: stimulation, hedonism | chance: 70
- G | scatter crumbs on the sill, keep very still, and wait for them to come | v: universalism (nature) | chance: 62
- W>B | count strictly by the rules and hand your sheet in first, to win | v: achievement | chance: 55
- U>R | make a hide out of a sheet and two chairs, and spy on the birds | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 72
- B>G | swap your sweets with your brother or sister for the last of the bird seed | v: universalism (nature) | chance: 78
- R>W | shout the whole family to the window when the woodpecker lands | v: benevolence, stimulation | chance: 72
- G>U | ask a grandparent the old country names for the birds, and write them in | door | v: tradition, self-direction | chance: 80
works: By the end of the hour the sheet has eleven kinds on it, and a robin came close enough to see its eye.
works: The sheet goes in on Monday, and the teacher pins it up on the classroom wall.
fails: A cat sits under the feeder all hour, and the sheet stays almost empty.
fails: {N} gets the birds muddled, and the sheet comes back covered in question marks.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- child: calls UB (cycle s3)

== a chess tournament in the school hall
tier: everyday
stages: child
age: 7-12
calls: UB
tone: mixed
life: school, play, friends
horizon: moment
roles: rival, friend, grandparent, an arbiter
earth: the chess club's Saturday tournament, with clocks, score sheets and a trophy the size of an egg cup
times: Rare: perhaps 1 in 20 children, a few times while in a chess club (estimate)
gap: 2-4
share: .05
rate: .5
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: Sixty children sit in rows in the school hall on a Saturday morning, every board set up the same, every clock ticking, and {N} is to move second against a boy in a bow tie.
scene W: Touch a piece and it must be moved; write down every move; shake hands before and after. {N} knows the rules by heart and means to keep every one.
scene U: {N} has spent a month on one opening from a library book and has a trap ready on move seven, if the other side walks into it.
scene B: The trophy goes to whoever wins most games, and {rival} is on the next table, grinning. {N} wants to be the one holding it.
scene R: The clock clacks, {Ns} knee jiggles under the table, and the urge to hurl every piece forward at once is almost too much to bear.
scene G: The old wooden set came from {grandparent} in a biscuit tin, and the pieces are worn smooth by forty years of fingers.
- W | resign politely the moment your queen is lost, as a good sport should | v: conformity, tradition | self_control: - | chance: 72
- U | spring the trap from the library book on move seven | v: achievement | chance: 50
- B | offer a draw while losing, with the face of someone who is winning | v: power, achievement | chance: 62
- R | throw every piece at the other king from the very first move | v: stimulation | chance: 45
- G | play slowly and steadily, the way a grandparent taught you on the old set | v: tradition | chance: 65
- W>R | stand up to the arbiter for a friend who swears the piece was never touched | v: benevolence | chance: 58
- U>G | replay the lost game slowly at home, until the loss stops stinging | v: self-direction | chance: 80
- B>W | give the noisy table your sweets to keep quiet, so the hall can think | v: conformity, security | chance: 70
- R>U | play the fastest, wildest games in the break, just to see what happens | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 85
- G>B | sit back, let the show-off attack, and wait for the blunder | v: achievement | chance: 65
works: {N} wins three games out of five and goes home with a certificate and the egg-cup trophy.
works: The trap works on move seven, and the other child's mouth falls open.
fails: The clock runs out with {N} a whole queen ahead, and the arbiter calls it a loss.
fails: {N} loses every game and rides home in silence with their face to the car window.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- child and juvenile: calls WR (cycle s4)

== practice before tea
tier: everyday
stages: child juvenile
age: 6-14
calls: WR
tone: mixed
life: home, school, play
horizon: week
roles: parent, friend, grandparent, a music teacher
earth: half an hour of violin practice before tea, the kitchen timer ticking and your friends outside
times: About 1 in 3 children, most days for years while they take music lessons (estimate)
gap: 0-.5
rate: 1
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
share: .33
scene: The violin case lies open on the bed, the kitchen timer is set for thirty minutes, and through the window {N} can hear {friend} kicking a ball against the garage doors.
scene W: The teacher's notebook says thirty minutes a day, scales first, and {parent} signs it every night. {N} hates to bring it back unsigned.
scene U: There is one bar in the new piece where the fingers tangle every single time. {N} slows it right down to see exactly where it goes wrong.
scene B: The grade one exam is in three weeks, and the child in the lesson before {Ns} passed it last term with a distinction.
scene R: {N} would rather play the tune from the television advert by ear, loud and wrong and glorious, than the same scale for the fortieth time.
scene G: The violin was {Ns} grandparent's, and the case still smells of rosin and the cupboard under their stairs.
- W | practise the full thirty minutes every day until the exam, timer and all | habit | v: conformity, achievement | self_control: + | chance: 68
- U | take the tangled bar at half speed, ten times over, until the fingers know it | v: achievement, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 75
- B | ask for a coin for every day of practice, and save every one | habit | v: achievement, security | self_control: + | chance: 88
- R | play the advert tune by ear, over and over, until every note is right | v: self-direction, hedonism | mark: learned a skill | self_control: + | chance: 85
- G | play the slow tune your grandparent used to hum, for nobody but yourself | v: tradition | chance: 70
- W>G | play the new piece to the grandparents on the phone every Sunday | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 85
- U>W | fill in the practice chart for days you never played | v: conformity | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: a forged chart; backfire: the teacher hears the truth in the first bar | self_control: - | chance: 78
- B>U | spend your birthday money on lessons with the better teacher across town | binds | v: achievement | mark: learned a skill | grants: musical instrument | chance: 62
- R>B | put your name down to play in assembly, to outshine the one who passed first | binds | v: achievement, stimulation | chance: 65
- G>R | take the violin out into the garden and play to the birds, for joy | v: hedonism, universalism (nature) | chance: 82
works: The tangled bar comes right at last, and the whole piece suddenly sounds like music.
works: At the next lesson the teacher listens to the end without a word, and then smiles.
fails: The strings squeal, the timer rings, and the piece is no better than yesterday.
fails: The lesson goes badly, and the notebook comes home with a sad face drawn in the margin.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- child and juvenile (12 and over): calls BR (cycle s1)

== fishing the canal with friends
tier: everyday
stages: child juvenile
age: 12-17
calls: BR
tone: joy
life: friends, nature, play
horizon: moment
roles: friend, parent, an older lad from the estate, a bailiff
earth: a summer evening fishing the canal with friends, rods tied to the bikes, a tin of sweetcorn and nobody's parents
times: Uncommon: perhaps 1 in 8 teenagers, a few summer evenings a year for a few years (estimate)
gap: 2-4
share: .12
rate: .5
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: Three bikes lie in the long grass by the lock, rods tied to the crossbars, and {N} and {friend} sit on the warm stone with a tin of sweetcorn and a bag of chips going cold. Somewhere under the brown water the carp are moving.
scene W: The sign on the lock says no fishing without a rod licence, and the bailiff walks this stretch on Sundays. {Ns} licence money is still in a jar at home.
scene U: {N} has watched the old match anglers at the bridge for weeks: they feed a little and often, and they fish the far bank under the trees.
scene B: An older lad from the estate says a big carp fetches good money from a man at the pub. {N} has never had that much money all at once.
scene R: The evening is hot, the lock gates are high, and {friend} is already pulling off a shirt and daring {N} to jump.
scene G: Swallows skim the water, a kingfisher flashes under the bridge, and the canal smells of warm stone and weed.
- W | buy a rod licence with your own money before casting a line | v: conformity, security | grants: fishing licence | chance: 92
- U | feed a little and often and fish the far bank, as the old men do | v: achievement | grants: hunting and fishing | chance: 62
- B | sell the big carp to the man at the pub for cash | v: power, achievement | mark: broke the law | closed: law: taking fish from a club water to sell; backfire: the bailiff takes names and the rods | chance: 50
- R | jump off the lock gate into the canal on a dare | body: heavy | v: stimulation | mark: took a wild risk | closed: approval: swimming at the locks is banned for a reason; backfire: a gash from a sunken trolley, or worse | death_if_fails: 0.001 | chance: 80
- G | sit on the warm stone until dark and watch the kingfisher come and go | v: universalism (nature) | chance: 65
- W>U | ask the old match angler at the bridge to show you how he plumbs the depth | v: self-direction | act: ask the old match angler at the bridge to show them how he plumbs the depth | chance: 50
- U>B | work out when the bailiff walks the stretch, and fish when he does not | v: power | mark: broke the law | closed: law: fishing without a rod licence; backfire: a fine, and the rod taken | chance: 80
- B>R | sell your old games to buy the best rod in the tackle shop window | v: hedonism, achievement | chance: 92
- R>G | stay out on the towpath with your friends until the bats come out | v: hedonism, universalism (nature) | chance: 62
- G>W | show the younger kids how to hold a fish so it swims away alive | v: benevolence, universalism (nature) | chance: 78
works: The float slides away under the far bank, and a fat golden carp comes up the net with the whole towpath cheering.
works: The evening ends with wet jeans, a sunburnt neck and a story that will last all summer.
fails: The bailiff comes along the towpath, takes everyone's names, and walks off with the rods.
fails: Three hours, two lost hooks, and nothing on the line but a crisp packet.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- juvenile and young adult: calls WB (cycle s2)

== a late raid with the online team
tier: everyday
stages: juvenile young_adult
age: 12-22
calls: WB
tone: joy
life: friends, play, home
horizon: moment
roles: friend, parent, sibling, players from other towns
earth: a Friday night raid in an online game with your team, headsets on and the house asleep
times: Many teenagers, most weeks; Pew 2024: 85 in 100 US teens play video games, about 4 in 10 daily
gap: 0-.75
scene: It is past eleven on a Friday, the house is dark, and {Ns} screen glows with five little portraits of friends from three different towns, all waiting on the same boss.
scene W: The team agreed the rules: everyone online at ten, nobody quits mid-fight, and loot goes to whoever needs it most. {N} set an alarm so as not to be late.
scene U: The boss has three phases, and {N} has watched every video of it there is. Phase two is where teams fall apart.
scene B: The rarest item in the game drops from this boss one time in fifty, and {N} wants it more than anything, and wants it to be theirs.
scene R: Headset on, heart pounding, {friend} yelling in one ear and the music thumping in the other: this is the best part of the whole week.
scene G: {friend} moved away two years ago, and this is the one place they still meet every week, in a forest that does not exist, laughing the way they used to.
- W | log on at ten as agreed and follow the raid leader's calls to the letter | v: conformity | chance: 85
- U | learn the boss's second phase and talk the team through it before the pull | v: achievement | chance: 70
- B | buy a stronger character with your parent's card, without asking | habit | v: achievement, power | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: a parent's card without asking; backfire: the bank statement, and the console locked in a cupboard | chance: 55
- R | play on until the sun comes up, though you promised to stop at one | habit | v: hedonism, stimulation | mark: broke your word | self_control: - | chance: 85
- G | leave the raid halfway and just talk with the old friend on voice chat | v: benevolence | mark: broke your word | closed: approval: the team's rule is that nobody quits mid-fight; backfire: the raid fails, and the team goes cold for a week | self_control: - | chance: 85
- W>B | offer to lead the raid, and keep the loot list fair and in your hands | v: power | chance: 55
- U>R | find a glitch that skips the boss's second phase, and try it live | v: stimulation | closed: approval: exploiting a bug; backfire: an account ban for a week | self_control: - | chance: 70
- B>G | trade your best item for the one your old friend has chased for months | v: benevolence | chance: 85
- R>W | hold on in the fight until three in the morning, so the team does not wipe | v: benevolence, conformity | self_control: + | chance: 55
- G>U | trust your gut on the pull instead of the guides, and learn from what happens | v: self-direction | chance: 55
works: The boss falls on the fourth try, and five voices scream together in {Ns} ears.
works: It is a ragged night of wipes and jokes, and {N} goes to bed grinning at three in the morning.
fails: The team wipes in phase two again and again, until someone quits in a temper.
fails: {parent} appears at the door in a dressing gown and pulls the plug out of the wall.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- juvenile and young adult: calls UG (cycle s3)

== two nights camping with friends by a lake
tier: everyday
stages: juvenile young_adult
age: 14-21
calls: UG
tone: joy
life: friends, nature, home
horizon: week
roles: friend, parent, a campsite warden
earth: two nights camping by a lake with friends, and no adults for miles
times: About 1 in 3 teenagers, once or a few times (estimate)
gap: 2-4
share: .33
rate: 1
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: The tent goes up crooked on the third try, the stove hisses, the beans catch on the bottom of the pan, and the lake turns pink and then grey as {N} and {friend} sit in the doorway with nobody to tell them when to sleep.
scene W: {N} promised {parent} two things: a text every night at nine, and nobody in the lake after dark. {N} gave their word on both.
scene U: {N} has the map, the forecast, three knots learned from videos and a checklist taped inside the lid of the food box. This trip will be done properly.
scene B: The best pitch, flat and right by the water, goes to whoever gets there first, and the families arrive after lunch.
scene R: No parents, no teachers, a lake, a pile of driftwood and a whole night ahead. {N} wants to do everything at once, starting with a fire.
scene G: An owl calls across the water, the fire pops, and the stars come out one by one until there are far too many to count.
- W | send the text at nine every night, exactly as promised | v: conformity, benevolence | mark: kept your word | tech: phone | chance: 90
- U | pitch the tent by the book, guy ropes pegged and a trench against the rain | v: achievement, security | mark: learned a skill | chance: 70
- B | grab the best pitch by the water before the families arrive | v: power, security | chance: 80
- R | build a roaring fire on the shore, though the site bans fires | v: stimulation, hedonism | closed: approval: the campsite bans open fires; backfire: the warden puts it out and sends everyone home a day early | chance: 62
- G | drag your sleeping bag out of the tent and sleep under the open sky | v: universalism (nature) | chance: 88
- W>R | ask the warden properly if the group may sit up by the fire past quiet hours | v: self-direction, conformity | chance: 55
- U>G | learn the stars from an app and name them for the others, one by one | v: universalism (nature), self-direction | tech: phone | chance: 75
- B>W | trade your biscuits to the family next door for a lend of their gas | v: security | chance: 65
- R>U | set off round the lake at dawn with no map, to see the far side | door | v: stimulation, self-direction | mark: took a wild risk | chance: 82
- G>B | catch a fish from the lake, cook it on the fire, and eat like kings | v: self-direction, hedonism | mark: broke the law | closed: law: fishing a private lake without a permit; backfire: the warden takes the rod and rings home | chance: 55
works: The tent holds through a night of rain, and in the morning the lake is as flat as glass.
works: They come home sunburnt, smoky and somehow two days older, with stories they will not tell their parents.
fails: The tent floods at two in the morning, and they spend the rest of the night shivering in the toilet block.
fails: A call to the warden, a call home, and a long silent drive back a day early.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- young adult and adult: calls WU (cycle s4)

== the Tuesday pub quiz
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult adult
age: 18-40
calls: WU
tone: joy
life: friends, play
horizon: week
roles: friend, rival, a quizmaster, the landlord
earth: the Tuesday quiz at the pub on the corner: six friends, one answer sheet, and a jackpot that rolls over
times: Some young adults, perhaps 1 in 5, most weeks for a spell; others never (estimate)
gap: 0-1.5
rate: 1
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
share: .2
scene: Six of them squeeze round the sticky table by the fruit machine with one pencil, one answer sheet and a bag of crisps split open in the middle, and the quizmaster taps the microphone. Round one: geography.
scene W: The team has rules: one answer per question, agreed by vote, and phones face down in the middle of the table. {N} keeps the sheet and keeps to them.
scene U: {N} has spent the week learning flags and capitals on the bus, and wants this geography round more than anything.
scene B: The jackpot has rolled over three weeks running, and the team that won it last month were checking their phones in the toilets. {N} saw them.
scene R: The music round is next, the drinks are cold, and {N} knows the answer to the first question before the quizmaster finishes reading it.
scene G: Same pub, same table, same faces every Tuesday for two years. The landlord starts pouring {Ns} drink before {N} reaches the bar.
- W | write down the answer the team votes for, even when you think it is wrong | v: conformity | mark: gave in to pressure | self_control: - | chance: 55
- U | spend the week learning capitals and flags on the bus | habit | v: achievement | mark: learned a skill | self_control: + | chance: 72
- B | insist the jackpot is split only among those who came every week | v: power | mark: made an enemy | chance: 75
- R | bet the team's whole kitty on the double-points round, on a hunch | habit | v: stimulation | mark: took a wild risk | chance: 50
- G | keep the same table and the same team every Tuesday, win or lose | habit | v: tradition, benevolence | mark: made a friend | grants: regular at the local | chance: 62
- W>G | sign the team up for the league, so the old crowd meets every week | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 85
- U>W | report the phone cheats to the quizmaster, with the times you saw them go | identity | v: conformity, universalism | mark: made an enemy | closed: approval: telling tales on regulars; backfire: the other team's friends turn the whole pub cold | chance: 68
- B>U | offer to buy the rounds if the clever stranger at the bar joins the team | door | v: achievement | mark: made a friend | chance: 65
- R>B | charm the landlord into a free round for the best team name | v: hedonism, power | chance: 88
- G>R | forget the score and sing along to the whole music round | v: hedonism | chance: 80
works: They win by half a point, and the whole table screams when the quizmaster reads out the scores.
works: Fourth place, a free round of drinks for the best team name, and a night nobody wants to end.
fails: Last place, and the wooden spoon goes on their table for the whole pub to see.
fails: A row breaks out over a wrong answer, and two of the team leave before the last round.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- young adult and adult: calls BR (cycle s1)

== a first half marathon
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult adult
age: 18-40
calls: BR
once: yes
tone: mixed
life: health, friends, play
horizon: week
roles: colleague, friend, parent, the other runners
earth: race day at your first half marathon, a number pinned to your shirt and thirteen miles ahead
times: Rare: perhaps 1 in 20 adults, once (estimate); US races counted about 2 million half marathon finishes a year in the mid-2010s (Running USA)
share: .05
rate: .5
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: Eight thousand runners shuffle at the start line with the sun already warm at nine, safety pins and water bottles everywhere, and {N} has a number on their chest and thirteen miles ahead.
scene W: Twelve weeks of the training plan are taped to the fridge, every run ticked, every rest day kept. Today {N} means to run exactly the pace the plan says.
scene U: {N} knows the course by heart: the hill at mile eight, the wind along the river, a water station every two miles. The watch is set to the second.
scene B: {colleague} bet {N} a month of coffees that they would never break two hours, in front of the whole office.
scene R: The gun goes, the crowd roars, the drummers under the bridge are booming, and {Ns} legs want to run as they have never run before.
scene G: The route follows the river under trees just turning, and the leaves crunch underfoot. {N} has run this path a hundred mornings, alone with the ducks.
- W | run the pace the plan says, mile after mile, whatever the others do | v: conformity, achievement | self_control: + | chance: 72
- U | run by the watch and the heart rate, and save it all for the hill | v: achievement | chance: 70
- B | skip the water stations on a hot morning to shave a minute off | body: heavy | v: achievement, power | death_if_fails: 0.001 | chance: 58
- R | go off with the fast runners at the gun and hang on for dear life | body: heavy | v: stimulation | self_control: - | chance: 50
- G | run for the river and the trees, and let the time be whatever it is | v: universalism (nature), hedonism | chance: 75
- W>U | join the running club and do the coach's Tuesday intervals all winter | habit | door | v: achievement | mark: learned a skill | title: club member | grants: keeping fit | chance: 68
- U>B | tuck in behind a tall runner along the river, out of the headwind | v: achievement | chance: 70
- B>R | pay extra for a place in the front pen, among the club runners | v: power, stimulation | chance: 82
